WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:03.900
Speaker 1: Hey, David, what happened to defund
the police?

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:04.960
Did they do it?

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.450
And why isn't anyone talking about it anymore?

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:12.559
Yeah, I don't know if the question is being
asked cynically or sarcastically or rhetorically.

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:14.120
So a bunch of different things happen.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:20.150
First of all, society has just sort of moved
on in some sense.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.490
And I don't mean altogether from defund the
police.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:27.970
There's still a ton of activism going on,
but it's not the lead story on CNN, MSNBC

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.150
and FOX every single day.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:34.969
It's not making headlines in the world's most
read, the country's most read newspaper.

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:38.530
So in a sense, the topic of defund the police
is simmer down.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.940
Now, you all know I was never for defund the
police.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.479
I was for how would I say it?

00:00:43.479 --> 00:00:50.679
I was for fund more crime prevention and better
trained police by providing the appropriate

00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:56.539
resources for the policing situation as judged
on an individual basis.

00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:57.629
That's a terrible tagline.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:00.529
I'm not a marketing guy, I admit that.

00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:06.240
But for me, the idea of eliminating or defunding
police never made sense.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:13.409
I laid out all the reasons why and and absent
some real way to prevent crime, defunding

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:17.439
police I just don't think is something that
that was ever going to really catch on.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:21.700
Now, what I do think is typically police are
responding to crime.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:28.000
So, sure, investigative units, joint terrorism
task force, FBI, CIA, you know, you've got

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.289
all these different entities and many of them
are working on preventing major crimes from

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:34.300
happening in advance.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.049
Police mostly are responding to crimes and
are not nearly as often in a situation where

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:40.229
they can prevent one.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:45.409
Sometimes police interfere or interject into
a situation where a crime has been committed

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:47.220
and they prevent further crime.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.210
So in a shooting where two people have been
killed, police show up, maybe nobody else

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.229
gets killed, which could happen if the shooter
is just left alone.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.829
But for the most part, police are not really
day to day street.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:04.670
Police are not preventing crime in the way
that maybe we would like to see happen.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:10.180
Access to social workers and mental health
services prevents crime, food stamps and anything

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:15.599
that makes people to be in a less desperate
situation where there might they might feel

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:19.680
compelled to commit a crime to feed their
family or themselves that prevents crime,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.900
good schools, prevent crime, prevent crime,
good public transit, so people can get to

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.760
where the jobs are that prevents crime, affordable
sports.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:37.980
And, you know, the way that we prevent crime
has less to do with police themselves, with

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.920
all of the exceptions that I listed.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:47.689
So I think that the focus should really be,
let's fund crime prevention and reform the

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:54.900
police with the 10 to 12 bullet point outline
that I've given in the past in terms of just

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.790
the dialog around defund.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.620
There are cities that recently have been seeing
more violence, not because they defunded.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.010
Now, this is really important.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.330
You see some Republicans, Don Junior, will
do this.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.990
He'll say look at what defund the police brought
to this city.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.720
And then you look and it's like, yeah, they
had to defund the police protest, but the

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.170
police weren't defunded.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.200
You can't blame defunding the police for crime
when the police were not defunded.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:26.069
I do think that more visible crime in some
cities over the last six months has probably

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:31.270
a little bit taken the wind out of the sails
of the straight up defund movement.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.629
It should do nothing at all to take our minds
off of the fund.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:39.360
Crime prevention and reform of the police
movement.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.420
That's where I've been for years on this issue.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.239
And I see more of my audience coming around
to it.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.760
And I think that that's a very good thing.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.870
Let me know what you think.

